WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:10.430
Flat on his back with a sinus infection at Oxford in March 1948, Pauling asked his

2
00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:16.090
wife to bring him paper and a slide rule, drew a polypeptide chain on an ordinary

3
00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:22.190
sheet, and folded it into a right-handed coil that came around every 3.6 residues

4
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:23.890
the alpha helix.

5
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:30.723
He had cracked it by throwing out the assumption everyone else clung to: that the

6
00:00:30.723 --> 00:00:34.663
coil had to close after a whole number of amino acids.

7
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:41.507
The following year, working with Harvey Itano, he ran sickle-cell and healthy

8
00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:47.247
hemoglobin through electrophoresis and watched them drift at different speeds the

9
00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:53.387
first time anyone pinned a specific human illness to a specific defect in a specific

10
00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:54.387
molecule.

11
00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:01.310
He called it a "molecular disease," and the phrase reframed medicine: a single

12
00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.490
changed amino acid could be the whole catastrophe.

13
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:11.610
Vernon Ingram would later locate the exact lesion one glutamate swapped for a valine

14
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:16.570
at the sixth position of the beta chain but the conceptual leap was Pauling's.

15
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:25.413
He holds two unshared Nobel Prizes, Chemistry and Peace, and proposed a triple helix

16
00:01:25.413 --> 00:01:31.853
for DNA that was wrong in nearly every particular, partly because he never saw the

17
00:01:31.853 --> 00:01:33.393
photographs at King's College.
